+# Contract: Command-Line Interface — feature 002 delta
+
+**Feature**: `002-password-reset` | **Date**: 2026-11-05
+
+This file is a **delta** on
+[feature 001's CLI contract](../../001-user-accounts-cli/contracts/cli-contract.md),
+which remains the contract for the five existing operations and is unchanged
+except for the two diagnostic strings in
+[§Changes to existing text](#changes-to-existing-text) below (A-025, FR-040).
+
+Like its predecessor it is asserted directly, by running the real executable in
+a subprocess with piped streams.
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m accounts --store PATH reset-token USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]
+python -m accounts --store PATH reset USERNAME
+```
+
+- `COMMAND` is now exactly one of `register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd`,
+  `delete`, `reset-token`, `reset` — seven names, nothing else (FR-020).
+- `--ttl SECONDS` is accepted **only** with `reset-token`, **not** abbreviated
+  (`--tt` is an error), and **not** more than once. Any of those three is a
+  usage error, status 2 (FR-021). `--ttl=SECONDS` is accepted as argparse's
+  standard equivalent form.
+- `SECONDS` must match `[0-9]{1,5}` and then fall in `1 … 86400`. Absent, it is
+  `900` (FR-021, FR-022, A-016).
+- `USERNAME` rules are unchanged: 3–32 characters of `[A-Za-z0-9_]`,
+  case-sensitive (FR-011).
+- There is still no `-h`/`--help` and no option abbreviation.
+- Neither a token nor a password may appear as an argument (FR-002, FR-023).
+
+## Standard input protocol
+
+| Command | Lines read | Order |
+|---|---|---|
+| `reset-token` | **0** | — must work with stdin closed (FR-023, scenario 1.6) |
+| `reset` | 2 | **token**, then **new password** (FR-023) |
+
+Both lines follow the existing line rules exactly (A-022, A-009, FR-014,
+SR-015): terminated by `\n` optionally preceded by `\r`, the terminator is not
+part of the value, a final line without a terminator is valid, at most 4 096
+bytes before the terminator, and valid UTF-8. A longer line is **refused, not
+truncated**.
+
+The token is compared **exactly as supplied** — no whitespace trimming, no case
+folding — so a line that does not match byte for byte is a wrong token (A-022).
+
+Lines beyond the second are never read and are not an error (FR-023).
+
+## Success output
+
+| Command | stdout | stderr | Exit |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `reset-token` | the token, 22 characters, followed by one `\n` — **and nothing else** | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| `reset` | `password reset` followed by one `\n` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+
+The issued token is the single deliberate exception to "no sensitive data in
+output" (SR-018): it is written only by `reset-token`, only on success, and only
+to stdout.
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+Still exactly the six of FR-003, with these additional conditions (FR-024).
+No status is redefined and no condition is removed.
+
+| Status | Additional condition for `reset-token` / `reset` |
+|---|---|
+| 0 | the token was issued, or the password was reset |
+| 1 | the supplied token is wrong, already used, replaced or expired — **or none was ever issued** (A-017) |
+| 2 | bad usage, invalid username, invalid `--ttl`, missing or over-long or non-UTF-8 input line, or a new password too short or too long |
+| 3 | the named account does not exist (A-021) |
+| 4 | **unreachable** from both new operations — a lockout does not restrict them (FR-024, FR-031, FR-039, A-018) |
+| 5 | the store could not be read, parsed or written |
+
+## Failure output
+
+One line to stderr, nothing to stdout (FR-026). New rows only; every row of
+001's table still holds for the five existing operations.
+
+| Status | Message to stderr |
+|---|---|
+| 1 | `invalid token` |
+| 2 | `usage: python -m accounts --store PATH {register\|login\|unlock\|passwd\|delete\|reset-token\|reset} USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]` |
+| 2 | `error: unknown command 'COMMAND': expected one of register, login, unlock, passwd, delete, reset-token, reset` |
+| 2 | `error: invalid ttl: expected a whole number of seconds from 1 to 86400, received 'VALUE'` |
+| 2 | `error: missing reset input: expected a token line then a new password line on stdin, received N line(s)` |
+| 2 | `error: token line too long: expected at most 4096 bytes before the line terminator` |
+| 2 | `error: token line is not valid UTF-8 text` |
+| 3 | `error: no such account 'USERNAME'` *(existing string, reused)* |
+
+Reused unchanged from 001 where the condition is the same: the invalid-username
+pair, `error: new password too short: …` / `too long: …`, the
+`password line too long` / `is not valid UTF-8 text` pair (for the **second**
+line), and every status-5 store message.
+
+`VALUE` and `USERNAME` are echoed through the existing sanitiser — any character
+outside printable ASCII becomes `\xNN` and the text is truncated at 64
+characters (SR-011, SR-018). `N` is a count of lines, never content.
+
+### `invalid token` is detail-free by requirement
+
+`invalid token` is an exact, fixed string with no further detail, like
+`invalid credentials` and `account locked` before it (FR-026). It is
+**byte-identical**, with an identical status, identical empty stdout, and
+comparable wall-clock time, across all five of:
+
+1. a value that was never issued;
+2. a token already redeemed;
+3. a token replaced by a later issuance;
+4. a token whose validity has elapsed;
+5. an account for which no token was **ever** issued.
+
+Timing parity is deliberate: every refused-token path spends exactly one scrypt
+derivation before answering, so the paths cannot be told apart by stopwatch
+either (SR-018, SC-018,
+[research R17](../research.md#r17--making-every-refusal-indistinguishable-including-by-stopwatch)).
+Nothing from the supplied token line is echoed to either stream.
+
+## Order of checks
+
+Fixed by FR-033 for `reset` and asserted as a precedence test. Full tables in
+[research R14](../research.md#r14--ordering-of-checks-for-the-two-new-operations).
+
+`reset`: username (2) → two input lines (2) → store loads (5) → account exists
+(3) → token current, unused and unexpired (1) → new password rules (2) → apply.
+
+`reset-token`: argument shape (2) → username (2) → `--ttl` value (2) → store
+loads (5) → account exists (3) → issue.
+
+Two consequences worth stating as contract, not implementation:
+
+- An **expired token with an 11-character new password** answers `invalid token`
+  with status 1, not a password error (scenario 3.4). The password line is read
+  before the store is loaded but judged after the token.
+- An **over-long token line** is status 2, decided before the store is opened,
+  so it is never compared against a record (spec edge case "Over-long token
+  line").
+
+## Store side effects
+
+| Command | Creates the store? | Writes the store? |
+|---|---|---|
+| `reset-token` | **never** (FR-032) | on success only |
+| `reset` | **never** (FR-038) | on success only |
+
+- Against a store path that does not exist, both return **3** and leave **no**
+  file behind — not the store, not a temporary file, not a lock file (FR-032,
+  FR-038).
+- Every failing invocation of either operation leaves the store **byte-identical**
+  (FR-027, SC-021). Unlike `login`, neither ever writes a failure count: a
+  refused token does not increment it and cannot lock the account (FR-039,
+  A-020).
+- A successful `reset` leaves every account other than the one being reset
+  byte-identical (FR-034, SC-021).
+- Both changes become visible all at once, under the existing atomic-replace and
+  directory-lock discipline (SR-016, SR-017). Two simultaneous redemptions of
+  one valid token yield exactly one success and one `invalid token` (FR-035,
+  SC-023).
+
+## Effect on the five existing operations
+
+Nothing changes except the two strings below (FR-040). In particular, with an
+outstanding token present:
+
+| Command | Effect on the token |
+|---|---|
+| `login` (success or failure) | **untouched** (FR-042, scenario 5.4) |
+| `unlock` | **untouched** (FR-042, scenario 5.4) |
+| `passwd` (success) | **invalidated** — refused afterwards with `invalid token` (FR-042, A-019, scenario 2.6) |
+| `passwd` (failure) | untouched; nothing is written |
+| `delete` (success) | removed with the account, so re-registering the username does not inherit it (FR-042, scenario 5.5) |
+
+### Changes to existing text
+
+Exactly two strings change, both of which enumerate the accepted operation
+names and would otherwise misreport the interface (A-025). These are the only
+permitted changes to any existing message, and both are emitted only on an
+argument-shape failure:
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| usage line | `usage: python -m accounts --store PATH {register\|login\|unlock\|passwd\|delete} USERNAME` | `usage: python -m accounts --store PATH {register\|login\|unlock\|passwd\|delete\|reset-token\|reset} USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]` |
+| unknown command | `error: unknown command 'X': expected one of register, login, unlock, passwd, delete` | `error: unknown command 'X': expected one of register, login, unlock, passwd, delete, reset-token, reset` |
+
+The second is generated from the command tuple and so follows it automatically.
+`tests/test_cli_contract.py` holds the two assertions that pin these strings and
+is updated to the new text; **no other assertion of feature 001 changes**
+(SC-019).
+
+## Worked example sequence
+
+Asserted line for line, status for status (SC-025). `$T` stands for the token
+captured from the `reset-token` invocation above it — it is read at runtime and
+never a literal in the repository (SC-015, secrets hygiene).
+
+```console
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s register alice
+registered alice                                      # stdout, exit 0
+$ python -m accounts --store ./s reset-token nobody < /dev/null
+error: no such account 'nobody'                       # stderr, exit 3
+$ python -m accounts --store ./s reset-token alice < /dev/null
+kZ8qf2mXr4TcN1pLbW9vdA                                # stdout, exit 0 — illustrative shape only
+$ printf '%s\nnew-password-here-21\n' "$T" | python -m accounts --store ./s reset alice
+password reset                                        # stdout, exit 0
+$ printf 'new-password-here-21\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s login alice
+ok                                                    # stdout, exit 0
+$ printf 'correct-horse-battery\n' | python -m accounts --store ./s login alice
+invalid credentials                                   # old password dead, stderr, exit 1
+$ printf '%s\nanother-password-21x\n' "$T" | python -m accounts --store ./s reset alice
+invalid token                                         # single use, stderr, exit 1
+$ python -m accounts --store ./s reset-token alice --ttl 1 < /dev/null
+pQ7wT3nZx8KcM2rBvY5sdF                                # stdout, exit 0
+$ sleep 2
+$ printf '%s\nyet-another-pass-21x\n' "$T2" | python -m accounts --store ./s reset alice
+invalid token                                         # expired, stderr, exit 1
+$ python -m accounts --store ./s reset-token alice < /dev/null
+hJ4yR9tVz6NdQ0mCxU8pwE                                # stdout, exit 0
+$ printf '%s\nelevenchars\n' "$T3" | python -m accounts --store ./s reset alice
+error: new password too short: expected at least 12 characters, received 11
+                                                      # stderr, exit 2
+$ printf '%s\ntwelvecharss\n' "$T3" | python -m accounts --store ./s reset alice
+password reset                                        # the token survived the refusal, exit 0
+$ python -m accounts --store ./s reset-token alice --ttl 0 < /dev/null
+error: invalid ttl: expected a whole number of seconds from 1 to 86400, received '0'
+                                                      # stderr, exit 2
+$ python -m accounts --store ./s reset-token alice --ttl 60 --ttl 60 < /dev/null
+usage: python -m accounts --store PATH {register|login|unlock|passwd|delete|reset-token|reset} USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]
+                                                      # stderr, exit 2
+$ printf '%s\nnew-password-here-21\n' "$T" | python -m accounts --store ./missing reset alice
+error: no such account 'alice'                        # stderr, exit 3
+$ ls ./missing
+ls: cannot access './missing': No such file or directory   # created nothing
+```
+
+The three token values shown are illustrative strings of the right shape, not
+issued tokens. The passwords are the suite's synthetic literals.
