+# Contract: Store File Format — feature 002 delta
+
+**Feature**: `002-password-reset` | **Date**: 2026-11-05
+
+A **delta** on
+[feature 001's store format](../../001-user-accounts-cli/contracts/store-format.md).
+Everything there still holds — encoding, write discipline, concurrency,
+permissions, size and count caps — except where restated below.
+
+The store remains a cross-invocation contract, and this feature makes it one in
+a second sense: `reset-token` writes the material that a *later* `reset`
+invocation verifies against, so the field rules below are what make the two
+operations compose at all (FR-028, FR-035, SR-016).
+
+## Encoding
+
+Unchanged:
+
+```python
+json.dumps(doc, ensure_ascii=True, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":"))
+```
+
+plus one trailing `\n`. Reset material is written and read **only** through this
+structured encoding, so no token value can create, merge or truncate a record
+(SR-011). No token value is ever written at all (SR-013).
+
+## Version
+
+**`version` stays exactly `1`.** It is deliberately not bumped. The change is
+additive and the new field is optional, so a store written before this feature
+and a store written by it are both version 1 and both load here. Bumping to `2`
+would make every pre-existing store unloadable and so would violate FR-041
+directly — the reasoning is in
+[research R18](../research.md#r18--store-compatibility-in-both-directions).
+
+Consequence, accepted by A-026: the **previous** build validates records against
+a closed eight-key set, so it reports a store carrying an outstanding token as
+`malformed` with status 5 rather than misreading it. Loud, not silent.
+Downgrading is not a supported operation.
+
+## Shape
+
+```json
+{
+  "accounts": {
+    "alice": {
+      "dklen": 32,
+      "failed_logins": 0,
+      "hash": "RUhBTVBMRS1ESUdFU1QtMzItQllURVMtQkFTRTY0LQ==",
+      "kdf": "scrypt",
+      "n": 131072,
+      "p": 1,
+      "r": 8,
+      "reset": {
+        "dklen": 32,
+        "expires_ms": 1762300000000,
+        "hash": "RVhBTVBMRS1UT0tFTi1ESUdFU1QtMzItQllURVMt",
+        "kdf": "scrypt",
+        "n": 131072,
+        "p": 1,
+        "r": 8,
+        "salt": "U1lOVEhFVElDLVRPS0VOLVNMVA=="
+      },
+      "salt": "U1lOVEhFVElDLVNBTFQtMTZC"
+    },
+    "bob": {
+      "dklen": 32,
+      "failed_logins": 0,
+      "hash": "RUhBTVBMRS1ESUdFU1QtMzItQllURVMtQkFTRTY0LQ==",
+      "kdf": "scrypt",
+      "n": 131072,
+      "p": 1,
+      "r": 8,
+      "salt": "U1lOVEhFVElDLVNBTFQtMTZC"
+    }
+  },
+  "version": 1
+}
+```
+
+`alice` has an outstanding token; `bob` has none and is byte-identical to a
+record written before this feature. Every digest and salt above is synthetic
+illustration material, not a real derivation (secrets hygiene).
+
+**Maximum nesting depth of a valid store becomes 4** (`document` → `accounts` →
+`record` → `reset`), up from 3. There is still no recursive structure, so parse
+cost remains linear in file size and no regular expression participates in
+parsing (SR-015).
+
+## Field rules
+
+### Document
+
+Unchanged: exactly `version` (integer, exactly `1`) and `accounts` (object,
+0–10 000 members, each key a valid username). Any additional key, missing key
+or wrong type → malformed, status 5.
+
+### Record
+
+Exactly the **eight** keys of feature 001, **plus** an optional `reset`. The
+field set is still an allow-list, not a minimum (SR-010):
+
+| Key | Required | Type | Accepted values |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `kdf` | yes | string | exactly `"scrypt"` |
+| `n` | yes | integer | power of two, `2 … 1048576` |
+| `r` | yes | integer | `1 … 32` |
+| `p` | yes | integer | `1 … 16` |
+| `dklen` | yes | integer | `16 … 64` |
+| `salt` | yes | string | padded standard-alphabet base64 decoding to exactly 16 bytes |
+| `hash` | yes | string | padded standard-alphabet base64 decoding to exactly `dklen` bytes |
+| `failed_logins` | yes | integer | `0 … 5` |
+| `reset` | **no** | object | the Reset table below |
+
+Accepted field sets are therefore exactly two: the eight required keys, or
+those eight plus `reset`. Any other key, a missing required key, or `reset`
+present with a non-object value → malformed, status 5.
+
+A record **without** `reset` means "no outstanding token" and is what every
+store written before this feature contains (FR-041).
+
+### Reset
+
+Exactly these eight keys, no more and no fewer:
+
+| Key | Type | Accepted values |
+|---|---|---|
+| `kdf` | string | exactly `"scrypt"` |
+| `n` | integer | power of two, `2 … 1048576` |
+| `r` | integer | `1 … 32` |
+| `p` | integer | `1 … 16` |
+| `dklen` | integer | `16 … 64` |
+| `salt` | string | padded standard-alphabet base64 decoding to exactly 16 bytes — **fresh per issued token**, never the record's password salt (SR-013) |
+| `hash` | string | padded standard-alphabet base64 decoding to exactly `dklen` bytes: `scrypt(token, salt, n, r, p, dklen)` |
+| `expires_ms` | integer | `1 … 253402300799000` — milliseconds since the Unix epoch |
+
+The first seven are the same names, types and ranges as the record's own
+password fields, which is why the verification routine is reused unchanged
+([research R12](../research.md#r12--what-is-recorded-against-the-account)).
+
+The `n * r` cross-field memory rule applies to the `reset` object exactly as it
+does to the record: parameters demanding more working memory than the tool's
+own `maxmem` (192 MiB) make the store malformed rather than an unbounded
+allocation (SR-015).
+
+`true`/`false` are **not** accepted where an integer is required — the check is
+explicitly `type(v) is int`, since Python's `bool` subclasses `int`.
+
+### Why `expires_ms` must be an integer
+
+SR-010 says a malformed expiry must not be treated as "not expired". `json`
+accepts and emits `NaN`, and every comparison with `NaN` is false, so a
+floating-point expiry field would make a hand-edited `NaN` read as *never
+expiring*. An integer with `type(v) is int` plus a range check has no such
+value. The millisecond unit is in the field name so no reader can compare it
+against `time.time()` by mistake; see
+[research R13](../research.md#r13--representing-the-expiry-instant).
+
+### Fixed size
+
+The serialised `reset` object is **174 bytes**, independent of the token, the
+username, and how many times a token has been issued (FR-043, SR-015). No
+account record can grow without limit, so the existing caps still bound the
+file. Measured worst case — 10 000 accounts each carrying reset material —
+serialises to 3 440 027 bytes against the 8 388 608-byte cap.
+
+## Validation discipline
+
+Unchanged in principle and extended in reach: every field of every `reset`
+object is validated against the table above on **load**, before any of it is
+used (SR-010). One invalid `reset` object fails the **whole** load with status
+5. It is never repaired, never partially loaded, never defaulted, never
+skipped, and the record it belongs to is never loaded without it.
+
+Specifically malformed (status 5), and each tested as such:
+
+- `reset` present but not a JSON object;
+- `reset` missing any of its eight keys, or carrying a ninth;
+- `expires_ms` absent, a string, a float, `true`, `NaN`, negative, zero, or
+  above `253402300799000`;
+- `reset.salt` or `reset.hash` of the wrong decoded length or outside the
+  base64 alphabet;
+- `reset.kdf` anything but `"scrypt"`; any scrypt parameter out of range or
+  failing the memory rule.
+
+Order of checks on load is unchanged (1 `open`, 2 `fstat` size cap before
+reading, 3 UTF-8 decode, 4 `json.loads` with `RecursionError` caught, 5 document
+shape → account count → every record). Step 5 now validates each record's
+`reset` object as part of validating that record.
+
+## Write discipline
+
+Unchanged, in full: `realpath` the store to find its own directory, create an
+unpredictably named `0o600` temporary file there with
+`O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_EXCL`, write, `fsync`, `close`, `os.replace` over the
+target, `fsync` the directory, and unlink the temporary on any failure
+(SR-012, SR-017).
+
+Additional guarantees for this feature:
+
+- **No token value reaches the temporary file.** It holds the same digests the
+  store does and nothing more (SR-013, SC-015).
+- **No other file is written.** No spool, no audit log, no sidecar, no
+  notification queue (SR-017, A-027).
+- **Neither new operation creates the store.** A missing file loads as an empty
+  in-memory store, both operations then fail with status 3, and nothing is
+  written (FR-032, FR-038).
+- **Only on success.** Both operations write exactly once, on success, and never
+  on any failure path — including a refused token, which unlike a failed `login`
+  records nothing at all (FR-027, FR-039).
+
+## Concurrency
+
+Unchanged: an exclusive `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX)` on a descriptor for the store's
+**directory**, held for the entire read–modify–write (SR-016). For this feature
+that single mechanism delivers three requirements with no new machinery:
+
+- the token is verified, the password replaced and the token consumed inside one
+  critical section, so **one token can never set two passwords** — two
+  simultaneous redemptions yield one success and one `invalid token` (FR-035,
+  SC-023);
+- two simultaneous issuances for one account serialise, and assignment to the
+  single `reset` key overwrites, so **two tokens are never outstanding** for one
+  account (FR-029, A-015);
+- the whole document is replaced atomically after an `fsync`, so a kill at any
+  point during issuance or redemption leaves the complete old or the complete
+  new store (SC-024).
+
+Rationale for locking the directory rather than the file is 001's
+[research R4](../../001-user-accounts-cli/research.md#r4--serialising-concurrent-invocations-sr-007-edge-case-simultaneous-invocations).
+
+## Compatibility summary
+
+| Direction | Behaviour | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| store written before this feature → this build | loads; every account simply has no `reset`; every password still verifies | FR-041, SC-020 |
+| store written by this build, no token outstanding | byte-identical to what the previous build would have written | FR-040, scenario 5.3 |
+| store written by this build, token outstanding → previous build | reported malformed, status 5 — loud, never misread | A-026 |
+| future version field | unchanged: reported malformed, status 5, never read best-effort | 001 contract |
